  "Chapter 12 set out the estimates of how many people lived in the Americas in 1492 (12.11); a 2019 synthesis by Koch and colleagues gives 60.5 million. [[c-gd-pop1492]] The same team estimates that European epidemics removed 90 percent of them within a century, a 'Great Dying'. [[c-gd-loss90]] Britannica gives a range of 50 to 95 percent by 1650 across the Americas, and more than 99 percent on most Caribbean islands (13.2). [[c-gd-brit-range]] Measles, smallpox, influenza, mumps, typhus and whooping cough were all new to the Americas. [[c-gd-diseases]]",
